Based on the provided context, it appears that AWS ECS is used for deploying containerized applications, not RDS (Relational Database Service). To clarify, here's how you can deploy a containerized web server using Amazon ECS and the AWS CDK on Fargate:

1. Install the AWS CDK toolkit: Follow the instructions in [Installing the AWS CDK](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cdk/latest/guide/getting_started.html) to set up your development environment.

2. Create a new project: Run `cdk init app --language typescript` to create a new AWS CDK project in TypeScript.

3. Import necessary modules: In the generated `lib/my-ecs-app-stack.ts` file, import the required modules for ECS and Fargate:

```typescript
import * as cdk from '@aws-cdk/core';
import * as ecs from '@aws-cdk/aws-ecs';
import * as ecsPatterns from '@aws-cdk/aws-ecs-patterns';
```

4. Define the VPC, cluster, and security group: In the `onCreate` method of your stack, define a VPC, create an ECS cluster, and set up a network load balancer with a security group:

```typescript
const vpc = new ecs.Vpc(this, 'MyVpc', { maxAzs: 2 });
const cluster = new ecs.Cluster(this, 'MyEcsCluster', { vpc });
const loadBalancedFargateService = new ecsPatterns.ApplicationLoadBalancedFargateService(this, 'MyWebServer', {
  cluster,
  cpu: 256,
  desiredCount: 1,
  taskImageOptions: { image: ecs.ContainerImage.fromRegistry('nginx') },
  publicLoadBalancer: true,
});
```

5. Add a custom container image (if needed): If you want to use a custom Docker image instead of the default nginx image, you can create and push your own image to Amazon ECR and update the `taskImageOptions` accordingly.

6. Synthesize and deploy: Run `cdk synth` to synthesize the CloudFormation template, then `cdk deploy` to deploy your resources to AWS.

For more information on using Docker Desktop with Amazon ECS, see [Docker Desktop integration with Amazon ECS](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/docker-cli-integration.html). However, as mentioned earlier, this is not for deploying a containerized application on RDS but rather for deploying containers to AWS ECS on Fargate using Docker tools.

To secure an Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) using IAM policies, you can follow these steps based on the provided AWS EC2 documentation:

1. **Create a custom policy for your VPC**
   - Navigate to the [IAM console](https://console.aws.amazon.com/iam/) and click on "Policies".
   - Click "Create Policy" and choose "JSON" as the policy type.
   - Paste the following JSON template into the editor, replacing `vpc-xxxxxxxx` with your VPC's ARN:

     ```json
     {
       "Version": "2012-10-17",
       "Statement": [
         {
           "Effect": "Deny",
           "NotAction": [
             "ec2:Describe*",
             "ec2:CreateVpc",
             "ec2:ModifyVpcAttribute"
           ],
           "Resource": ["arn:aws:ec2:*:*:vpc/*"],
           "Condition": {
             "StringNotEquals": {
               "aws:ResourceTag/Name": [
                 "your-vpc-name"
               ]
             }
           }
         },
         {
           "Effect": "Allow",
           "Action": [
             "ec2:Describe*",
             "ec2:CreateVpc",
             "ec2:ModifyVpcAttribute"
           ],
           "Resource": ["arn:aws:ec2:*:*:vpc/your-vpc-name"]
         }
       ]
     }
     ```

   - Click "Review Policy" and give your policy a name, then click "Create Policy".

2. **Attach the custom policy to an IAM role**
   - Navigate to the [IAM console](https://console.aws.amazon.com/iam/) and click on "Roles".
   - Select the role that you want to attach the policy to, or create a new one if necessary.
   - Click "Permissions" > "Attached policies", then click "Add permissions".
   - Search for your custom policy by name, select it, and click "Review".
   - Review the policy details and click "Attach Policy".

By following these steps, you have created a custom IAM policy that restricts access to your VPC while allowing specific actions on your named VPC. This helps secure your AWS VPC by controlling who can describe, create, or modify VPC resources.

[Source: aws/EC2/Example policies for working with the AWS CLI or an AWS SDK<a name="ExamplePolicies_EC2"></a>]

Based on the provided context, here's a step-by-step guide to configure IAM permissions for Google Cloud Pub/Sub using Terraform:

1. First, ensure that you have enabled the necessary APIs and services in your Google Cloud project. You can do this by navigating to the Google Cloud Console (https://console.cloud.google.com/) and enabling the Pub/Sub API.

2. Install Terraform if you haven't already: https://www.terraform.io/downloads.html

3. Create a new directory for your Terraform project and initialize it:

   ```
   mkdir terraform-gcp-pubsub && cd terraform-gcp-pubsub
   terraform init
   ```

4. Create a `variables.tf` file to define the necessary variables:

   ```hcl
   variable "project_id" {
     description = "The ID of the Google Cloud project."
   }

   variable "topic_name" {
     description = "The name of the Pub/Sub topic."
   }

   variable "subscription_name" {
     description = "The name of the Pub/Sub subscription."
   }
   ```

5. Create a `main.tf` file to define the resources:

   ```hcl
   provider "google" {
     project = var.project_id
     region  = "us-central1" # Replace with your desired region
   }

   resource "google_pubsub_topic" "example" {
     name = var.topic_name
   }

   resource "google_pubsub_subscription" "example" {
     name  = var.subscription_name
     topic = google_pubsub_topic.example.id
   }

   # Grant the Pub/Sub Admin role to a service account
   data "google_service_account" "pubsub_admin" {
     account_id  = "<SERVICE_ACCOUNT_ID>" # Replace with your desired service account ID
     project_id  = var.project_id
   }

   resource "google_project_iam_member" "pubsub_admin" {
     role      = "roles/pubsub.admin"
     member    = "serviceAccount:${data.google_service_account.pubsub_admin.email}"
     project   = var.project_id
   }
   ```

6. Replace `<SERVICE_ACCOUNT_ID>` with the ID of a service account that you want to grant Pub/Sub Admin role. You can create a new one using the following command:

   ```
   gcloud iam service-accounts create PUBSUB_ADMIN_SA --project=PROJECT_ID
   ```

7. Apply the Terraform configuration:

   ```
   terraform apply -var "project_id=PROJECT_ID" -var "topic_name=TOPIC_NAME" -var "subscription_name=SUBSCRIPTION_NAME"
   ```

To implement auto-scaling for Google Cloud Storage, you can use Cloud Functions and Pub/Sub to trigger scaling based on changes in the storage bucket. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a Google Cloud project (if not already created) and enable the required APIs:
   - Go to the [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/)
   - Click on your project name, then select "Select a project" and choose or create a new project.
   - Enable the following APIs: Cloud Functions, Pub/Sub, and Storage.

2. Create a storage bucket:
   - In the Cloud Console, go to the Cloud Storage browser.
   - Click on "Create Bucket," enter a unique name for your bucket, and click "Create."

3. Create a topic in Pub/Sub:
   - Go to the Cloud Pub/Sub page in the Cloud Console.
   - Click on "Create Topic," enter a unique name for your topic, and click "Create."

4. Create a Cloud Function that triggers when an object is added to the storage bucket:
   - Go to the Cloud Functions page in the Cloud Console.
   - Click on "Create Function," select a trigger as "Cloud Storage - Object Finalize," choose your project and storage bucket, and click "Create."
   - Write the function code in Node.js (or any other supported language) that publishes a message to the Pub/Sub topic when an object is added to the storage bucket. Here's an example using Node.js:

```javascript
exports.objectCreated = functions.storage.object.finalize(async (obj, context) => {
  const pubsub = context.eventContext.pubsub;
  const topic = pubsub.getTopic('my-topic');
  await topic.publish({ data: 'New object added to the bucket.' });
});
```

5. Create a Cloud Function that scales based on messages received from Pub/Sub:
   - Go back to the Cloud Functions page, click on "Create Function," select a trigger as "Pub/Sub - Message received," choose your project and topic, and click "Create."
   - Write the function code in Node.js (or any other supported language) that scales based on the number of messages received from Pub/Sub. Here's an example using Node.js:

```javascript
const { CloudFunctions } = require('@google-cloud/functions');
const functions = new CloudFunctions();

exports.scale = async (event, context) => {
  const instanceManager = functions.getInstanceManager();
  const numMessages = event.data.data.length;
  if (numMessages > 10) {
    await instanceManager.addInstance();
  } else if (numMessages < 5) {
    await instanceManager.removeInstance();
  }
};
```

6. Deploy the Cloud Functions:
   - Click on "Deploy" for both functions, and follow the instructions to deploy them.

Now, when an object is added to your storage bucket, a message will be published to the Pub/Sub topic, triggering the scaling function to adjust the number of instances based on the number of messages received.
